  <main>
    <div class="mast"><span>Study 01b · 11 October 2026</span><span>Calibration mark / three frames</span></div>
    <section class="heading">
      <h1>Same mark,<br><em>three frames</em></h1>
      <p class="intro">An AIC PhD Target appears below a kylix fragment, then beside both sides of P.Mich. inv. 6215i. The printed credit repeats. Whether each frame contains the same physical target is not recorded here.</p>
    </section>

    <p class="method">Three complete photographs, independently fitted to the same window. Clay rectangles locate the printed target credit; no shared object scale is implied.</p>
    <section class="trio" aria-label="Three photographs containing the AIC PhD Target">
      <figure>
        <div class="eyebrow"><span>01 / Kylix fragment</span><span>Met record 671161</span></div>
        <div class="plate">
          <img src="record.jpg" alt="Attic kylix fragment photographed above its accession label and calibration strip.">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 599 603" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="475" y="536" width="123" height="67"/></svg>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="caption"><strong>Record photograph / target below the label</strong>The Met lists 2011.604.1.1114, the number printed on the paper label.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <div class="eyebrow"><span>02 / P.Mich. 6215i</span><span>Recto / Fig. 1</span></div>
        <div class="plate">
          <img src="pmich-6215i-recto.jpeg" alt="The recto of a papyrus fragment, photographed with color patches, a circular target, ruler, AIC target mark, and a dated inventory card.">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 714" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="440" y="611" width="110" height="103"/></svg>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="caption"><strong>Article photograph / target below the papyrus</strong>The card reads “AFTER 8/18/2023” and gives the same P.Mich. inventory number.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <div class="eyebrow"><span>03 / P.Mich. 6215i</span><span>Verso / Fig. 2</span></div>
        <div class="plate">
          <img src="pmich-6215i-verso.jpeg" alt="The verso of the papyrus, photographed turned with its dated inventory card and calibration strip along the right edge.">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 714 1000" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="611" y="455" width="103" height="115"/></svg>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="caption"><strong>Article photograph / target at the right edge</strong>The same credit turns with the strip in this view of the reused side.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section" aria-label="Enlarged crops of the repeated target credit">
      <div class="detail-head"><h2>The small inscription</h2><span class="meta">Source details / verso turned for reading</span></div>
      <div class="details">
        <figure><div class="detail-stage"><img src="target-kylix.jpg" alt="Enlarged AIC PhD Target credit from the kylix photograph."></div><figcaption class="detail-label">Kylix / lower right</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><div class="detail-stage"><img src="target-recto.jpg" alt="Enlarged AIC PhD Target credit from the papyrus recto photograph."></div><figcaption class="detail-label">Recto / lower right</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><div class="detail-stage"><img src="target-verso.jpg" alt="Enlarged AIC PhD Target credit, turned horizontal for reading, from the verso photograph."></div><figcaption class="detail-label">Verso / rotated to read</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="reading">
      <p>The little credit holds steady while the photographed things change: terracotta, papyrus, then the other side of that papyrus. It gives the frames a common mark, not a documented route between collections.</p>
      <p class="small">The papyrus article describes the verso as turned 90 degrees and reused. I can see the target in both photographs. I cannot tell whether these images show one physical calibration tool or several copies of the same design.</p>
