        <figure><div class="detail-stage"><img src="target-kylix.jpg" alt="Enlarged AIC PhD Target credit from the kylix photograph."></div><figcaption class="detail-label">Kylix / lower right</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><div class="detail-stage"><img src="target-recto.jpg" alt="Enlarged AIC PhD Target credit from the papyrus recto photograph."></div><figcaption class="detail-label">Recto / lower right</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><div class="detail-stage"><img src="target-verso.jpg" alt="Enlarged AIC PhD Target credit, turned horizontal for reading, from the verso photograph."></div><figcaption class="detail-label">Verso / rotated to read</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="reading">
      <p>The little credit holds steady while the photographed things change: terracotta, papyrus, then the other side of that papyrus. It gives the frames a common mark, not a documented route between collections.</p>
      <p class="small">The papyrus article describes the verso as turned 90 degrees and reused. I can see the target in both photographs. I cannot tell whether these images show one physical calibration tool or several copies of the same design.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="sources" aria-label="Sources and image credits">
      <p>Kylix photograph: Metropolitan Museum, <em>Terracotta fragment of a kylix</em>, record 671161; the AIC PhD Target carries its own ©2011 AIC mark. <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/671161">Collection record ↗</a></p>
      <p>P.Mich. inv. 6215i, recto and verso: figures 1–2 in Maria Nowak and W. Graham Claytor, “A Local Will with a ‘Codicillary’ Clause,” <em>Pylon</em> 4 (2023). Images courtesy of the University of Michigan Library Digital Collections; article licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. <a href="https://doi.org/10.48631/pylon.2023.4.101705">Article ↗</a></p>
      <p>Detail images are crops from those photographs. The verso crop is rotated for reading; no image has been color-corrected.</p>
    </section>
    <footer><span>Study 01b / apparatus kept in view</span><a href="edge.html">Study 01c / The apparatus turns ↗</a><a href="index.html">The rest of the cup ↗</a><a href="after.html">Probably after ↗</a></footer>
